TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design – Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Orbit and Ground Control (Images 1–5); STK files not evident"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Whole document"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Mission Design – Launch","issue":"Equatorial launch is argued to be most efficient, but Earth-rotation benefit largely disappears for a retrograde ~98° orbit. Plane-change penalty from 5°N is not analysed."},{"where":"Orbit – orbital elements table","issue":"RAAN is said to indicate the launch facility location. RAAN depends on launch time/epoch and site longitude; no epoch is given. Table also uses Earth radius 6371 km, inconsistent with STK's 6378 km."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Sun-synchronous daytime imaging is claimed but local time of descending node is never specified. Orbit period, swath width and ground sample distance are not calculated, so 600 km and 'daily' coverage are unsupported."},{"where":"Orbit / Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The only orbital manoeuvre is said to occur at EOL, yet the satellite is stated to have no thrusters, so manoeuvre claims are contradictory."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"A five-year life at 600 km with passive disposal is asserted without decay or lifetime analysis. STK run 2025–2030 is mentioned but no results are shown; executive summary cites perturbation calculations that are absent."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Drag-sail sentence is truncated ('to ‘ and is suitable...'), leaving the EOL description incomplete and the drag sail unsized."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Access table lists two entries numbered 13 and 14 rows in total despite text claiming fifteen periods; pass frequency per day is not stated explicitly."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mount Pleasant radio telescopes are used as an Earth-observation downlink without frequency, link budget, data rate or antenna suitability justification."},{"where":"Orbit – Image 3","issue":"Inclination-altitude plot has no source citation and appears to be borrowed material."},{"where":"Mission Design – Launch","issue":"Wikipedia is used as a source ('Timeline of spaceflight'), which the task explicitly prohibits."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"In-text citations run to [74] but only [1]–[10] are listed; [10] is used for both the Falcon 9 manifest and the drag-sail reference; entries [5] and [10] are garbled."}],"overall":"Plausible single-satellite sun-synchronous concept with correct 600 km inclination (about 97.8°) and STK access analysis. However, launch reasoning is inverted for retrograde orbit, RAAN and manoeuvre statements are wrong or contradictory, EOL is truncated and unsupported, and citations are broken and include Wikipedia. Approximately 50% weighted.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Primary mission, rationale (bleaching, fluid lensing), single-spacecraft choice and customers are all addressed with reasoned detail. The operator is vague ('operated and accessed from Australia ground facilities') and funding is speculative. Introduction references are present, but the bibliography is URL-only and incomplete, so referencing is only partly adequate.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Strengths: SSO inclination for 600 km (about 97.8°) checks out, and STK access data and orbit screenshots are provided. Weaknesses: equatorial-launch logic is inverted for a retrograde orbit; the Vega E is unbuilt; the RAAN explanation is wrong; no local time of descending node, period or swath/GSD sizing; manoeuvre statements contradict; no decay analysis; EOL drag-sail text is truncated; the access table is mis-numbered.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Design – Launch","quote":"The most efficient rocket launches are from locations close to the equator as this is where maximum rotational velocity can be experienced [5]."},{"where":"Mission Design – Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle"},{"where":"Orbit – orbital elements table","quote":"The initial value indicates the location of the launch facility."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"the only orbital manoeuvre occurs at the EOL segment of the satellite's mission."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"However, the ZYZZsat is without thrust capability and so must use a ‘passive’ strategy."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"The first fifteen access periods are listed above."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion draws some reasonable points, including the launch-vehicle risk and the ground-station choice. However, it largely restates the design without quantitative evidence such as contact durations, pass counts or EOL lifetime. It does not acknowledge the unresolved issues in the body, so it is somewhat generic.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A specific launch vehicle – the Vega E – has been selected although it has not been built yet."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Headings and a clear section structure with an executive summary are present, and most figures carry captions. Citations are seriously inconsistent: in-text numbers run to [74] but the bibliography lists only [1]–[10], [10] is used twice, entries are garbled, and Wikipedia is cited. Frequent grammar and typo errors, unnumbered tables and an uncaptioned Image 3 further reduce quality.","evidence":[{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Mission Design – Launch","quote":"It’s first launch will occur in 2024"},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Mission Design – Launch table","quote":"February (TBD)"}]}]}
